d with the Monte-Carlo SD of τ̂ in place of SE_HC0; if κ\\* barely moves, the SE is not the issue. *Predictions (falsifiable)*: (1) smooth mechanisms: κ\\* ≤ 2 for the median mechanism × d, with a frozen κ̂ fixed on the original cells before the b\n+**C9 — An assumption-indexed extrapolation-sensitivity interval (Brief P6, rev. 11; exploratory-after-results on the existing cells, confirmatory on the shared block).** *Order of events, which the paper tells as it happened:* P5(ii) was predicted to work and failed; the non-identification argument was formulated afterwards as an explanation of the failure; P6 and P7 are the tests of that explanation, not something the pre-registration anticipated. The interval is the honest-CI form of Armstrong–Kolesár 2018/2020 (worst-case bias over a derivative-bounded class, rule-of-thumb M̂) with κ × the in-support magnitude as the relative-magnitudes logic of Rambachan–Roth 2023 (abstract only read); **not claimed as new**; what is new is its application to the extrapolation functional of exposure regressions (corner offsets c(p), degree dependence), the controls and κ*. Definition (frozen in the Brief): τ̂ ± cv_α(κB/SE)·SE_HC0, α = 0.10, B₁ = range of τ̂ over the ladder, B₂ = ½·M̂·((1−p)²+p²) with M̂ the largest |∂²/∂f²| of a global cubic in f (with z, z×f) over the observed range of f; κ grid {0.25, …, 64} all reported. *Main quantity:* **κ\\*** per mechanism × d, the smallest κ with mean coverage ≥ 0.90 (∞ if none): how much assumption each mechanism needs, **net of SE miscalibration** (HC0 undercovers with no bias at high leverage and small n): κ\\* is also computed on the zero-bias null cells (s = 0) as κ\\*₀ and reported raw and as κ\\* − κ\\*₀, and on a subset recomputed with the Monte-Carlo SD of τ̂ in place of SE_HC0; if κ\\* barely moves, the SE is not the issue. *Predictions (falsifiable)*: (1) smooth mechanisms: κ\\* ≤ 2 for the median mechanism × d, with a frozen κ̂ fixed on the original cells before the b\n+- **Rev. 1